e Council  on Foreign Relations and joins us now  from Washington DC welcome to DW  um in recent weeks we've seen Russia  take steps to escalate the conflict the  illegal annexation of four more  Ukrainian regions the mobilization of  new troops can I ask you what do you  think uh Putin will do next  well the Putin has tried to do with  these annexations attempted annexations  one has to say is to draw a wet line  around these territories and to prevent  exactly what is happening right now The  Columns of those troops so here's  different options that he can now pursue  one option is to send more and more  Russian men into the area and to try to  stabilize it which will be difficult the  other is to escalate further with the  tax on Ukrainian civilian infrastructure  with the tax on Western for instance  energy infrastructure to make clear that  his threat that those attempted Annex  territories belong to Russia has indeed  not been a bluff as he said in his  speech  Germany's foreign minister said today  that we should take Russia's nuclear  threats seriously how serious do you  think they are  we should take those threats seriously  they are part of the options that the  Russian president has on the table in  this war but until we come to that  maximum option which is also for Russia  would mean a lot of costs and a lot of  disadvantages there will be other steps  that we will see so it is an option but  it is an unlikely option at least for  the moment because there are just too  many other options that the Russian  president canned War to escalate and to  scare Ukraine and the West away from  further offenses well that's the thing  isn't it you hear the word nuclear and  everyone gets very worried so even it is  an unlikely scenario what can the West  do I mean can it do anything to prevent  nuclear escalation by Putin  what the West can do is to Signal very  clearly to put in what the costs of such  an action would be and that is something  which is not only up